      "players": "3 players in a queue (one working, two waiting); 2 coaches.",
      "equipment": "1 tackle donut, 3 cones, and a clear 10 × 8 m working area.",
      "instructions": "Set up the players in a line behind the two back cones. The first player moves towards the middle cone on the CHASE call, then settles into a ready position on CHILL. Once the player is balanced and watching, the coach sends the donut on a slow, predictable diagonal roll. The player tracks it, adjusts their feet, makes a controlled tackle and stops the roll. On RESET, release the pad and get up. On RETURN, loop back outside the working lane while the coaches recover the donut; the next player moves to the front. One player goes at a time.",
      "coaching": "CHASE — eyes on the donut; close the distance without sprinting blindly.\nCHILL — shorten the steps, keep the feet moving and get into a balanced, low position with eyes up.\nKILL — meet the moving pad under control, keep the head safely to the side, make shoulder contact and wrap with the arms. Don't dive or lead with the head.\nRESET — release, regain your feet and clear the lane.\nCoach one thing at a time. If the approach is messy, slow the roll down.",
      "regression": "Start with the donut stationary, or roll it very gently over a short distance. Bring the player closer and practise getting into position and wrapping before adding a moving target.",
      "progression": "Vary which side the donut travels towards, or change the roll angle between turns. Have the tackler read the line and adjust their feet rather than guessing. Keep the roll slow enough for a safe tackle; only add speed when technique stays consistent.",
      "questions": "When did you need to slow your feet? Where were you looking as the donut moved? Did you get close enough to wrap, or reach and dive? Where was your head at contact? Were you in control when you released?",
